      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The choice of antiplatelet therapy after acute coronary syndrome (ACS) is complicated: Ticagrelor and prasugrel are novel alternatives to clopidogrel, patients with some genotypes may not respond to clopidogrel, and low-cost generic formulations of clopidogrel are available."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the most cost-effective strategy for dual antiplatelet therapy after percutaneous coronary intervention for ACS."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Decision-analytic model."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DATA SOURCES",
          "content": "Published literature, Medicare claims, and life tables."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "TARGET POPULATION",
          "content": "Patients having percutaneous coronary intervention for ACS."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "TIME HORIZON",
          "content": "Lifetime."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "PERSPECTIVE",
          "content": "Societal."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "Five strategies were examined: generic clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor, and genotyping for polymorphisms of CYP2C19 with carriers of loss-of-function alleles receiving either ticagrelor (genotyping with ticagrelor) or prasugrel (genotyping with prasugrel) and noncarriers receiving clopidogrel."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Direct medical costs, quality-adjusted life years(QALYs), and incremental cost-effectiveness ratios (ICERs)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS OF BASE-CASE ANALYSIS",
          "content": "The clopidogrel strategy produced$179 301 in costs and 9.428 QALYs. Genotyping with prasugrel was superior to prasugrel alone, with an ICER of $35 800 per QALY relative to clopidogrel. Genotyping with ticagrelor was more effective than genotyping with prasugrel ($30 200 per QALY relative to clopidogrel). Ticagrelor was the most effective strategy($52 600 per QALY relative to genotyping with ticagrelor)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS OF SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS",
          "content": "Stronger associations between genotype and thrombotic outcomes rendered ticagrelor substantially less cost-effective ($104 800 per QALY). Genotyping with prasugrel was the preferred therapy among patients who could not tolerate ticagrelor."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "LIMITATION",
          "content": "No randomized trials have directly compared genotyping strategies or prasugrel with ticagrelor."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Genotype-guided personalization may improve the cost-effectiveness of prasugrel and ticagrelor after percutaneous coronary intervention for ACS, but ticagrelor for all patients may bean economically reasonable alternative in some settings."
        }
      ]},
